% Fire alarm: sprinklers and an alarm (Drools examples, fire/simple)
%
% The Logical English (for LPS) twin of a Drools rule base, written by the DRL translator
% from LPS2's reading of Fire.drl (kept in sources/).
% Each Drools rule is a reactive rule. Its facts are states of the world, and what it does to
% working memory (insert, modify, delete) is something happening in the world, with its causal law.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 30.

the events are:
    the fire in *a room* is put out; known as fire_is_put_out.
    a fire starts in *a room*; known as fire_starts.

the actions are:
    the alarm goes off; known as alarm_goes_off.
    an alarm goes on; known as alarm_goes_on.
    the sprinkler in *a room* turns off; known as sprinkler_turns_off.
    the sprinkler in *a room* turns on; known as sprinkler_turns_on.

the fluents are:
    an alarm is on; known as alarm.
    there is a fire in *a room*; known as fire.
    there is a room called *a name*; known as room.
    there is a sprinkler in *a room*; known as sprinkler.
    the sprinkler in *a room* is on; known as sprinkler_on.

the knowledge base fire_alarm includes:

the text of the rule base is at "sources/Fire.drl".

% Rule "RaiseAlarm"; its last condition keeps it from firing again while what it brings about holds, as Drools fires a rule once; its Java, not translated: System.out.println( "Raise the Alarm");
if there is a fire in a room
    and it is not the case that an alarm is on
then an alarm goes on.

when an alarm goes on
then an alarm is on.

% Rule "CancelAlarm"; its Java, not translated: System.out.println( "Cancel the Alarm");
if it is not the case that there is a fire in a room
    and an alarm is on
then the alarm goes off.

when the alarm goes off
then it is not the case that an alarm is on.

